<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sam Nujoma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/2025">2025</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sam Nujoma (1929–2025) was a Namibian nationalist leader and the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, from independence in 1990 to 2005. He co-founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> in 1960 and led its long struggle against South African rule from exile, including the armed struggle that began in 1966. As president he followed a policy of national reconciliation and kept a mixed economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sam-nujoma.jpg" alt="Sam Nujoma" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ana Nascimento/ABr · CC BY 3.0 br · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sam_Nujoma_(2004)_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samuel Shafiishuna Daniel Nujoma was born on 12 May 1929 at Etunda, a village in Ongandjera, near Okahao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovamboland">Ovamboland</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a>. He was the eldest of eleven children, and his mother, Helvi Mpingana Kondombolo, was a Uukwambi princess by descent. He tended the family's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, attended a Finnish missionary school at Okahao from the age of ten and completed Standard Six, the highest level open to black pupils at the time.</p><p>In 1946 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a>, where he worked in a general store and later at a whaling station. In 1949 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>, working as a cleaner for South African Railways while attending night school. He became involved in politics in the early 1950s through trade unions, shaped by his experience of the contract labour system, and was dismissed from the railways in 1957.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1959 Nujoma co-founded the Ovamboland People's Organization and was elected its first president. After the Old Location Massacre in December 1959 he was arrested for organising the resistance, and in February 1960 he went into exile to lobby the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. The organisation was reconstituted as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">South West Africa People's Organisation</a> (SWAPO) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 19 April 1960, with Nujoma elected president in absentia. He built its headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>, and in 1962 SWAPO founded an armed wing, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>.</p><p>The first armed clash with South African forces took place on 26 August 1966 at Omugulugwombashe and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war">Namibian War of Independence</a>. Nujoma's role in the army has been questioned following allegations of torture and imprisonment of fighters in the Lubango dungeons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, where about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> members were held for over nine years. He led SWAPO's negotiators over the proposals that became United Nations Security Council Resolution 435 in 1978, and a ceasefire with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was signed on 19 March 1989. After 29 years in exile he returned in September 1989.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The Constituent Assembly elected in November 1989 chose Nujoma as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>'s first president. He was sworn in on 21 March 1990, proclaimed a policy of national reconciliation to improve relations among the country's racial and ethnic groups, and began a land reform programme in 1990. He was re-elected in 1994, and the constitution was changed so that he could stand for a third term in 1999. His successor, Hifikepunye Pohamba, took office on 21 March 2005, and Pohamba also succeeded him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> president in 2007. In December 2005 Parliament conferred on him the title Founding Father of the Namibian Nation. He published his autobiography, Where Others Wavered, in 2001, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a> on 8 February 2025, aged 95.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sam Nujoma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Namibia became independent on 21 March 1990, with <b>Sam Nujoma</b> as first president.</blockquote><a href="/state/namibia">Namibia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 1988 New York Accords, UN-supervised elections were held, and Namibia became independent on 21 March 1990 under President <b>Sam Nujoma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-west-africa">South West Africa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ongandjera, in what is now <a href="/countries/namibia">Namibia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/namibia">Namibia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-17.88,15.07&amp;year=1991" aria-label="Ongandjera on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4959 2827 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sam Nujoma"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5409 3097h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5409 3097h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-17.88,15.07&amp;year=1991">What was here in 1991? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
